      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Delta(9)-Tetrahydrocannabinol (1 and 5 mg/kg, i.p.) produced, dose-dependently, antinociceptive effects using hot plate and tail flick tests in rats. These effects were suppressed not only by the cannabinoid CB(1) receptor antagonist SR 141716A (0.5 mg/kg; i.p.) but also by the dopamine D(2) receptor antagonists S(-)-sulpiride (5 and 10 mg/kg; i.p.) and S(-)-raclopride (1.5 and 3 mg/kg; i.p.). Conversely, Delta(9)-tetrahydrocannabinol antinociceptive effects were potentiated by the dopamine D(2) receptor agonists (-)-quinpirole (0.025 mg/kg, s.c.) and (+)-bromocriptine (0.5 and 1 mg/kg; i.p.). Our results indicate that the antinociceptive effects of Delta(9)-tetrahydrocannabinol are mediated by the concomitant activation of cannabinoid CB(1) and dopamine D(2) receptors and that dopamine D(2) receptor agonists may be useful in improving the analgesic effects of cannabinoids."},
